# RULE (ARCHITECTURE.md §6.3.2; bugs.md 46):
#   KeychainSecretStore's thread guard sits ABOVE the probe latch as well as
#   above the probe, so all four of its methods enforce the same contract:
#   asked off the application thread they refuse, available() answering the
#   bool spelling — false — of the NoBackend the other three return.
#
# GUARD: tst_keychainlive::everyOperationRefusesToRunOffTheApplicationThread
#
# HARNESS: needs a real Secret Service. Every case in tst_keychainlive is gated
#   on a backend answering and a QSKIP exits 0, so this driver — which runs the
#   binary with no keyring and no LOFTAIL_TEST_KEYCHAIN — would report the
#   mutation SURVIVED when it was never executed. Kill it by hand, or in the
#   keychain-live CI job's environment:
#     packaging/test-keychain/run-keychain-tests.sh --build build
#   Verified red there, on the two assertions the fix exists for.
#
# SILENT WHEN BROKEN: a store that has already probed answers its latch from any
# thread, which is an unsynchronised read of a plain bool pair another thread
# wrote — the only race in the class, in its only method that did not enforce
# its own contract. Nothing in the shipped application can see it, because every
# route reaches this object through secretStore(), which marshals; what it costs
# is that the guarantee is a call-site discipline rather than the class's, and a
# later caller that reaches the store directly gets a keychain answer computed
# on a thread with no event loop and no prompter to show an unlock dialog to.

diff --git a/src/core/KeychainSecretStore.cpp b/src/core/KeychainSecretStore.cpp
--- a/src/core/KeychainSecretStore.cpp
+++ b/src/core/KeychainSecretStore.cpp
@@ -233,12 +233,12 @@
     // it is the bool spelling of the NoBackend the other three refuse with, and no
     // production caller can see it, every route reaching this object through
     // secretStore(), which marshals to the application thread.
-    if (!onTheRightThread())
-        return false;
-
     if (m_probed)
         return m_available;
 
+    if (!onTheRightThread())
+        return false;
+
     m_probed = true;
 
     // The cheap negative first — but ONLY as a negative. QKeychain::isAvailable() answers
